artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let the spacetime geometry of the observable universe be modelled, on sufficiently large scales, by a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric

  ds² = -c² dt² + a(t)² [ dr²/(1-kr²) + r²(dθ² + sin²θ dφ²) ],

which presupposes that the spatial sections are exactly homogeneous and isotropic. Let δρ(x; R) denote the fractional fluctuation of the smoothed matter density field at position x on a smoothing scale R, and let N(<R) denote the mean count of galaxies within balls of radius R centered on galaxies, with fractal/correlation dimension D₂(R) = d log N(<R) / d log R. The FLRW assumption requires the existence of a finite homogeneity scale R_H such that for all R ≥ R_H one has D₂(R) → 3 and ⟨|δρ(x;R)|²⟩^{1/2} → 0 in a statistically well-defined sense, with the distribution of galaxies and clusters approaching spatial randomness on scales R ≳ R_H.

The problem is the following: determine whether the actual large-scale matter distribution of the universe is consistent with this FLRW homogeneity-and-isotropy hypothesis, taking into account the simultaneous observational facts that

(a) coherent structures (walls, filaments, voids) appear with apparently fractal-like scaling and inhomogeneities persist at least up to scales of order 100–200 Mpc;
(b) the local Hubble law v = H₀ d is observed to be remarkably linear already in the distance range roughly 2–25 Mpc, where the matter distribution is manifestly inhomogeneous (the so-called inhomogeneity paradox);
(c) large-scale coherent peculiar velocity (bulk-flow) fields are detected on scales of tens to hundreds of Mpc;
(d) reports of quasi-periodic spatial structure ("periodic galaxy walls") and of redshift periodicities for galaxies and quasars exist;
(e) the observed near-isotropy of the cosmic microwave background, by itself, does not imply spatial homogeneity, since it is also compatible with a spherically symmetric inhomogeneous (e.g. Lemaître–Tolman–Bondi) cosmology in which the observer sits near a centre of symmetry.

Specifically: prove or disprove that there exists a finite scale R_H beyond which the observed galaxy/matter distribution is statistically homogeneous and isotropic in the sense above, so that the FLRW metric is a legitimate background description of the universe on scales R ≥ R_H.

